 "existing_tips": [
  "Modals (vouloir, pouvoir, devoir) take the infinitive directly. je veux manger (I want to eat), il p",
  "'Je veux bien' is a polite way to accept an offer. It's softer than 'oui' and means 'yes, gladly' or",
  "'Pouvoir' + infinitive = can/able to. 'Tu peux venir?' = Can you come? Informal question via intonat",
  "`on` is the all-purpose informal 'we' in spoken French: `on va au cinéma` = 'we're going to the movi",
  "In French, age uses 'avoir' (to have): j'ai 20 ans = I am 20 years old. Literally 'I have 20 years'.",
  "For -er verbs ending in -cer (commencer), add a cedilla before -ons: nous commençons. This keeps the",
  "'Demander' means 'to ask for' – no extra preposition needed. 'Vous demandez l'addition' = You ask fo",
  "'Être en retard' = to be late. French uses the preposition 'en' before 'retard', not 'à' or nothing.",
  "'Habiter à' is used with cities: 'habiter à Paris'. For countries, use 'habiter en' (feminine) or 'h",
  "In French, 'dans' is used for enclosed spaces: 'dans un bureau' (in an office). Use 'à' for cities a",
  "Aimer is a regular -er verb. Use aimer + definite article for general likes: J'aime le café (I like ",
  "`vous` is both 'you (plural)' and 'you (formal singular)'. Context decides. With strangers, in busin",
  "`on` also = English 'one' or 'they' (impersonal): `on dit que...` = 'they say that...'. Two meanings",
  "'Rendre visite à' means 'to visit a person'. Don't confuse with 'visiter' which is for places: je vi",
  "'Du' (de + le) is the masculine partitive article meaning 'some'. Use for uncountable nouns: du café",
  "'Détester' = to hate. With abstract nouns, French requires the article: 'il déteste le bruit' (he ha",
